wait():-makes the parent process wait until any one of its child processes finishes.
wait(NULL);
The parent does not continue until one child terminates.We cannot choose which particular child wait() waits for.

waitpid():-is used when we want the parent to wait for a specific child process.
waitpid(pid[2], NULL, 0);
Here, the parent specifically waits for Child 3.

Difference between wait and waitdpid:-
wait()                                  waitpid()
Waits for any child process             Waits for a specific child
Cannot select a particular child        Can select a particular child using PID
Simpler to use                          Gives more control
Returns when any child terminates       Returns when the specified child terminates

Zombie Process – Short Theory
A zombie process is a child process that has finished execution, but its parent has not collected its exit status.
- It remains in the process table.
- It can be created when the parent does not use wait() or waitpid().
- Calling wait() or waitpid() removes the zombie by collecting the child's status.
